> primroot 1
the smallest primitive root mod 1 is 0
  how: (Z/1)* is cyclic of order phi = 1 = 1; g generates it iff g^(1/p) != 1 for each prime p | 1
  phi(1) = 1 primitive roots:
  check: the powers 0^0 .. 0^0 are 1 distinct units
> primroot 2
the smallest primitive root mod 2 is 1
  how: (Z/2)* is cyclic of order phi = 1 = 1; g generates it iff g^(1/p) != 1 for each prime p | 1
  phi(1) = 1 primitive roots: 1
  check: the powers 1^0 .. 1^0 are 1 distinct units
> primroot 4
the smallest primitive root mod 4 is 3
  how: (Z/4)* is cyclic of order phi = 2 = 2; g generates it iff g^(2/p) != 1 for each prime p | 2
  phi(2) = 1 primitive roots: 3
  check: the powers 3^0 .. 3^1 are 2 distinct units
> primroot 7
the smallest primitive root mod 7 is 3
  how: (Z/7)* is cyclic of order phi = 6 = 2 * 3; g generates it iff g^(6/p) != 1 for each prime p | 6
  phi(6) = 2 primitive roots: 3 5
  check: the powers 3^0 .. 3^5 are 6 distinct units
> primroot 8
there is no primitive root mod 8: the unit group has exponent lambda(8) = 2 < phi(8) = 4, so it is not cyclic
  primitive roots exist only mod 1, 2, 4, p^k and 2p^k (p an odd prime)
> primroot 11
the smallest primitive root mod 11 is 2
  how: (Z/11)* is cyclic of order phi = 10 = 2 * 5; g generates it iff g^(10/p) != 1 for each prime p | 10
  phi(10) = 4 primitive roots: 2 6 7 8
  check: the powers 2^0 .. 2^9 are 10 distinct units
> primroot 100
there is no primitive root mod 100: the unit group has exponent lambda(100) = 20 < phi(100) = 40, so it is not cyclic
  primitive roots exist only mod 1, 2, 4, p^k and 2p^k (p an odd prime)
> primroot 3 mod 7
yes: 3 is a primitive root mod 7 (its order is 6, phi(7) = 6)
  how: 3^(6/2) = 6, 3^(6/3) = 2 (a primitive root has none of these equal to 1)
  check: the powers 3^0 .. 3^5 are 6 distinct units
> primroot 2 mod 7
no: 2 is not a primitive root mod 7 (its order is 3, phi(7) = 6)
  how: 2^(6/2) = 1, 2^(6/3) = 4 (a primitive root has none of these equal to 1)
> primroot 5 mod 11
no: 5 is not a primitive root mod 11 (its order is 5, phi(11) = 10)
  how: 5^(10/2) = 1, 5^(10/5) = 3 (a primitive root has none of these equal to 1)
